course: dictionary
lesson: 3056
title: Short word: ECG
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: ECG is a short word, called an abbreviation. It is short for electrocardiogram, which is a long name for a heart test. Short words like this help doctors and nurses talk quickly.
explain: «People cannot feel other animals' electric fields, but our hearts and brains work with electric signals that doctors measure with an ECG and an EEG.» The clue is the word measure: an ECG measures the electric signals that the heart makes.
explain: ECG can mean the test or the machine that does the test. In a hospital drama, a wavy line beeping on a screen shows a patient's heartbeat. Read the words around ECG to decide which meaning fits.
fact: ECG is short for electrocardiogram.
fact: An ECG is a noun, so you can say «have an ECG» or «an ECG machine».
fact: An ECG records the electrical signals of the heart as a wavy line.
fact: ECG can also mean the machine that does the test, which is called an electrocardiograph.
fact: Abbreviations are short forms of longer words, and you can often work out the long form from the clues in the text.
exercise: «People cannot feel other animals' electric fields, but our hearts and brains work with electric signals that doctors measure with an ECG and an EEG.» What does ECG stand for? | Electronic calendar | *Electrocardiogram | Electrical comedy | Electric cardigan
exercise: In a hospital drama, a wavy line beeps on a screen. What is the screen showing? | A dog's bark | *A patient's heartbeat | A weather map | A football score
exercise: Tom has to have an ECG at the hospital. What is an ECG? | A cooking machine | A bone X-ray | *A test that records the heart's electrical signals as a wavy line | A bus timetable
test: ECG is short for which longer word? | *Electrocardiogram | Electron | Electrolyte | Electricity
test: In «ECG machine», what does the word machine tell you about ECG? | It is a feeling | *It is the thing that does the test | It is a kind of food | It is a type of weather
exam: In the phrase «have an ECG», what kind of word is ECG? | An adjective | A full stop | *A noun | A verb
